{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1251\uc1 \deff0\deflang1033\deflangfe1049{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fcharset0\fprq2{\*\panose 02020603050405020304}Times New Roman;}{\f1\fswiss\fcharset0\fprq2{\*\panose 020b0604020202020204}Arial;}{\f33\froman\fcharset238\fprq2 Times New Roman CE;}{\f34\froman\fcharset204\fprq2 Times New Roman Cyr;}{\f36\froman\fcharset161\fprq2 Times New Roman Greek;}{\f37\froman\fcharset162\fprq2 Times New Roman Tur;}{\f38\froman\fcharset186\fprq2 Times New Roman Baltic;}{\f39\fswiss\fcharset238\fprq2 Arial CE;}{\f40\fswiss\fcharset204\fprq2 Arial Cyr;}{\f42\fswiss\fcharset161\fprq2 Arial Greek;}{\f43\fswiss\fcharset162\fprq2 Arial Tur;}{\f44\fswiss\fcharset186\fprq2 Arial Baltic;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}{\stylesheet{\qj\sb120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \lang2057\cgrid \snext0 Обычный;}{\s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang2057\kerning28\cgrid \sbasedon0 \snext0 заголовок 1;}{\s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang2057\cgrid \sbasedon0 \snext0 заголовок 2;}{\*\cs10 \additive Основной шрифт;}}{\info{\title Press release}{\author Simon Clarke}{\operator Vadim}{\creatim\yr1998\mo12\dy7\hr13\min28}{\revtim\yr1998\mo12\dy7\hr17\min16}{\printim\yr1998\mo12\dy7\hr16\min48}{\version3}{\edmins1}{\nofpages2}{\nofwords667}{\nofchars3805}{\*\company ISITO}{\nofcharsws4672}{\vern73}}\paperw11909\paperh16834\margl1440\margr1440 \widowctrl\ftnbj\aenddoc\lytprtmet\hyphcaps0\formshade\viewkind1\viewscale90\viewzk2\pgbrdrhead\pgbrdrfoot \fet0\sectd \psz9\linex0\headery706\footery706\colsx709\endnhere\sectdefaultcl {\*\pnseclvl1\pnucrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl2\pnucltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl3\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl4\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl5\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl6\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl7\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl8\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl9\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang2057\kerning28\cgrid {New forms of employment and household survival strategies in the Russian transitional economy
\par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang2057\cgrid {Scientific-practical seminar reporting results of new research conducted by ISITO
\par }\pard\plain \qj\sb120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \lang2057\cgrid {The research aimed to answer the question how do Russians survive? Everybody has an answer to this question: Russians survive because there is an enormous `hidden economy\rquote  which does not appear in official statistics, they have undeclared incomes from secondary employment, they produce most of their own food, they receive gifts from friends and relatives. But none of these opinions are based on serious research. This project is the first to try to answer these questions on the basis of the most advanced methods of sociological research.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang2057\cgrid {The key findings of the research are as follows:
\par }\pard\plain \qj\sb120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \lang2057\cgrid {\i New private sector employment}{ has grown rapidly since 1994, and now accounts for around 20% of jobs in the large cities. The new private sector is complementary to rather than competitive with state and former state enterprises. The new private sector has made a large contribution to the development of trade and services but has made little progress in industry or even in construction and transport. Most new private enterprises operate within the framework of the law, in the sense that their regular employees are usually registered as such, even if not all incomes are declared for tax purposes \endash  the exceptions are mostly individual and family businesses. 
\par Around 20% of the population is involved from time to time in }{\i secondary employment}{, which makes a significant contribution to household income, but particularly for those who are already privileged. The informal economy is mostly confined to the sphere of secondary employment which provides a flexible reserve of casual labour for the private trade and service sectors. 
\par Production of food in }{\i subsidiary agriculture}{ does not lead to any reduction in spending on food, savings on potatoes and vegetables being used to buy more fruit, meat and dairy products. Subsidiary agriculture absorbs a very large amount of time and substantial amount of money for rather limited results. The poor do not have the time and money to engage in subsidiary agriculture, which makes little contribution to the survival of Russian urban families.
\par Disadvantaged households are disadvantaged on all dimensions: they have fewer members in primary jobs, fewer opportunities for secondary employment, less possibility of growing their own food, fewer friends and relatives able to help. Ten per cent of the Russian population were on the brink of survival, even before the recent crisis.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang2057\cgrid {\page Policy implications
\par }\pard\plain \qj\sb120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \lang2057\cgrid {The vast majority of households depend for the vast majority of their incomes on earnings from state and former state enterprises and organisations, on the one hand, and social benefits, particularly pensions, on the other. Neither new private sector employment, secondary employment, subsistence production and private transfers can do any more than supplement the traditional forms of income and employment. This means that the first priority of labour and social policy should be to improve earnings and employment prospects in state and former state enterprises and organisations and to maintain the real value and regular payment of social benefits, above all pensions.
\par The development of the new private sector should be encouraged as a complement rather than a competitor to the state sector. There is no evidence that the use of illegal forms of employment is associated with greater employment flexibility. Labour and tax law should be applied uniformly to all enterprises, to prevent the unscrupulous minority from gaining a competitive advantage by refusing to meet their legal obligations.
\par The revision of the Labour Code can most usefully focus on making legal provision for casual, part-time and secondary employment, without undermining the security and basic rights of people in their primary jobs.
\par Government should encourage the effective reform of commercial agriculture and develop an effective system of social assistance for the poor rather than subsidise petty agricultural production. 
\par The government should think very hard before it decides to take account of the availability of land as a criterion of eligibility for social assistance. Equally, the possibility of a family receiving support from friends and relatives should not be considered in assessing eligibility for social assistance. 
\par The most important way of helping people to survive is by helping them to help themselves by ensuring the creation of jobs and the efficient operation of the labour market.
\par 
\par  
\par 
\par }}